  "body": "Source of the \"comment end state\" contains this _invisible_ comment about potential security issue if EOF steps are omitted:\n\n``` html\n<dt>EOF</dt>\n   <dd><span>Parse error</span>. Switch to the <span>data state</span>. Emit the comment token.\n   Reconsume the EOF character.</dd> <!-- For security reasons: otherwise, hostile user could put a\n   <script> in a comment e.g. in a blog comment and then DOS the server so that the end tag isn't\n   read, and then the commented <script> tag would be treated as live code -->\n```\n\nWe with @inikulin tried to find out how can this be an issue, but didn't find any way to trick tokenizer into the described behavior even if the state is not changed on EOF, and neither see how change of the state after the EOF can mitigate any kind of security issues as such state is further ignored by tree construction or any other caller anyway.\n\nDoes anyone know where this comment is just a legacy thing or, if not, how to reproduce the specified behavior and which exact step is that warning related to (\"parse error\", \"switch to the data state & reconsume\" or \"emit the comment token\")?\n\nIf it's obsolete, would like to remove it to avoid future confusion, and if not, worth clarifying and putting in a real visual `note` to warn spec readers.\n\n/cc @zcorpan @annevk @domenic \n",
